<Base title="technical breakdown · keel" description="keel's plain-language claims with their real names: what ships, the three rules that can say no, and what keel leaves out on purpose.">
  <header class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">technical breakdown</span>
      <h2>The same ideas, with their real names</h2>
      <p class="sub">This page covers what the rest of the site says in plain words,
      with the terms, mechanisms, and tradeoffs. Under this page, the source of truth is
      <a href="https://github.com/redlinelabs-dev/keel">the repo</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section id="box">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">the machinery</span>
      <h2>What ships, precisely</h2>
      <div class="specs">
        <div class="spec">
          <h3 id="ingest">ingest boundary</h3>
          <p>After every <code>WebFetch</code>, <code>WebSearch</code>, and MCP result,
          keel appends a note marking the content as untrusted <strong>data, never
          instructions</strong>. That makes indirect prompt injection harder. It doesn't
          prevent it.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>It's advisory context, not enforcement.</li>
            <li>Content fetched with plain <code>curl</code> through Bash isn't covered.</li>
            <li>It flags zero-width characters and bidirectional overrides.</li>
            <li>It only reads. It never blocks a call or changes a result.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="spec">
          <h3 id="guard">security guard</h3>
          <p>A <code>PreToolUse</code> hook. It blocks commands that do irreversible damage,
          and it returns the documented <code>permissionDecision: "ask"</code> before
          commands shaped like exfiltration.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Text versus code.</strong> A Dockerfile in a heredoc that mentions
            <code>rm -rf</code> is text. The same text piped into <code>bash</code> is code,
            and the guard tells them apart.</li>
            <li><strong>Fails closed.</strong> If the policy is broken, every mutating tool
            (Bash, Edit, Write) is blocked. Reads stay open.</li>
            <li><strong>Your policy wins.</strong> <code>~/.config/keel/policy.json</code>
            can add rules, or exempt shipped ones with <code>bash.allow</code> and
            <code>paths.allow</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Private keys are off limits to the file tools.</strong> That covers
            the usual names: <code>id_*</code>, <code>*.pem</code>, <code>*.key</code>,
            and <code>~/.aws/credentials</code>. A key with an unusual name, like a bare
            <code>deploykey</code>, needs a line in <code>paths.zeroAccess</code>.
            <code>paths.confirmRead</code> turns the block into a prompt, and the block
            message says how.</li>
            <li><strong>Aliases don't get around it.</strong> For covered paths, the file
            tool check follows symlinks and matches hardlinks by inode. Running
            <code>ln</code> on a key prompts in Bash before the link exists.</li>
            <li><strong>Bash is the backup net.</strong> Shell reads such as
            <code>cat</code>, <code>cp</code>, <code>openssl</code>, and
            <code>tar ~/.ssh</code> prompt. So do node, python, ruby, and perl when they
            name a key. An unusual enough command can still get through. The file tool
            check is the guarantee.</li>
            <li><strong>Edits to the policy file prompt.</strong> The file tools prompt
            at whatever path <code>KEEL_POLICY_FILE</code> names. Shell and interpreter
            writes prompt too: a redirect, <code>sed -i</code>, <code>node -e</code>,
            <code>ruby -e</code>. This check runs before the policy's own allow list, so
            a policy that allows everything still can't approve its own rewrite. A shell
            path hidden behind a variable can get through. The file tool check catches
            it.</li>
            <li><strong>Off switch.</strong> <code>KEEL_GUARD_OFF=1</code> turns all of it
            off.</li>
            <li><strong>Not a sandbox.</strong> Claude Code has its own OS-level
            <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sandboxing">Bash sandbox</a>, off
            until <code>/sandbox</code> or <code>sandbox.enabled</code> turns it on. keel
            neither installs nor enables it. It limits the writes and network hosts of
            Bash, PowerShell, and Monitor commands and their child processes, using
            Seatbelt on macOS and <code>bubblewrap</code> plus <code>socat</code> on Linux
            and WSL2. Read, Edit, and Write go through permissions instead, and hooks and
            MCP servers run outside it. By default it still lets commands read
            <code>~/.ssh</code> and <code>~/.aws/credentials</code>. The guard is a
            separate layer, a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook on Bash, Read, Edit, and
            Write.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="spec">
          <h3 id="trailers">commit hygiene</h3>
          <p>Blocks unwanted attribution trailers in commit messages. The list lives in
          <code>KEEL_BLOCKED_TRAILERS</code>, and <code>KEEL_TRAILERS_OFF=1</code> turns
          it off. It fails open on any internal error. It only inspects real
          <code>git commit</code> invocations, so <code>git log --grep</code> still
          works.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="spec">
          <h3 id="activity">activity log</h3>
          <p>An append-only monthly JSONL file under the config directory. It's written
          from documented hook payloads (<code>UserPromptSubmit</code>,
          <code>Stop</code>) and never by parsing transcripts. File names include the
          device, so two machines never write to the same file. It makes no model calls.
          Hooks capture, and skills interpret. <code>KEEL_ACTIVITY_OFF=1</code> turns it
          off, and <code>KEEL_ACTIVITY_DIR</code> moves it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="spec">
          <h3 id="recall">query recall</h3>
          <p>A <code>UserPromptSubmit</code> hook that scores the fact files under
          <code>&lt;config dir&gt;/projects/*/memory/</code> against your prompt. Rare
          words count for more than common ones, stemming is crude, and there's a
          relevance floor. It adds up to three matches, each citing its file, inside a
          <code>&lt;keel-memory&gt;</code> block marked as data. It only reads local
          files, has a 1.2 second budget, and adds nothing to most prompts on purpose.
          <code>keel recall "…"</code> shows what a prompt would get, and
          <code>KEEL_RECALL_OFF=1</code> turns it off.</p>
          <p id="hindsight-recall">With <code>KEEL_HINDSIGHT_URL</code> set, a second
          <code>UserPromptSubmit</code> hook sends the prompt to the bank's recall
          endpoint (world facts, low budget, embeddings plus a cross-encoder, no LLM
          call). It skips prompts with fewer than three topic words, keeps facts the
          cross-encoder scores 0.5 or more, and gives up at 2 seconds. Each fact comes
          back as one dated line citing its source document, inside a
          <code>&lt;keel-hindsight&gt;</code> block marked as data. Any failure adds
          nothing. <code>keel doctor</code> times it against the deadline, and
          <code>KEEL_HINDSIGHT_RECALL_OFF=1</code> turns it off.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="spec">
          <h3 id="memory-wiring">memory wiring</h3>
          <p>Third-party memory engines plug in as bridge plugins that declare MCP
          servers in <code>.mcp.json</code>, which Claude Code documents. keel ships no
          vector store, no LLM call, and no daemon. <code>keel doctor</code> checks the
          wiring, down to whether the engine's project mapping points at the store you
          configured.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="spec">
          <h3 id="cli">the cli</h3>
          <p><code>keel status · log · recall · doctor · setup · settings · update ·
          migrate · link</code></p>
          <p>Mostly read-only and flags first. Its writes are idempotent and go only to
          the <code>KEEL_*</code> keys it owns, plus, with your explicit consent, the
          <code>permissions</code> rules <code>keel settings --yes</code> appends. It
          backs up the previous state before its first write. It's on the Bash tool's
          PATH, not your shell's, so in practice Claude runs it for you.
          <code>keel update</code> checks the commit SHA and ignores the version
          string.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="constitution">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">the constitution</span>
      <h2>Three rules that can say no</h2>
      <div style="margin-top:1.6rem">
        <div class="rule">
          <div class="num">01</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Own your config, buy your capabilities</h3>
            <p>Sync, guardrails, and setup are configuration. They're personal, nobody
            else can ship them, and they're a few hundred lines. Memory, recall, and
            reflection are capabilities. They're general, useful to strangers, and people
            who work on them full time already build them better. During design, "should
            I build this?" turned into "someone already did" six separate times. What was
            left to own is small and unglamorous.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="rule">
          <div class="num">02</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Documented surfaces only</h3>
            <p>Claude Code once shipped about 20 releases in a single week. A layer under it survives only
            by depending on what Anthropic documents and treats as a contract. The system
            keel replaced parsed session transcripts, an undocumented format. It worked,
            and one format change would have broken it without a sound. If it isn't in
            the docs, keel doesn't read, write, or parse it. The exceptions are repairs,
            listed with their blast radius and how keel stops needing them. <em>An
            exception you state is a debt. An exception you deny is a lie.</em></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="rule">
          <div class="num">03</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Admission by felt need</h3>
            <p>Prose gets in freely, because removing it is <code>rm</code>. A test gets
            in when it catches a kind of failure that already happened in this repo.
            Runtime code, meaning hooks, subcommands, and anything a session executes,
            needs a real user to have felt the need at least once, on a real machine.
            "Another harness found it useful" is an argument from someone else's life.
            Anything that would fail silently when removed ships with the check that
            would notice.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top:1.4rem">The full text is in the repo as
      <a href="https://github.com/redlinelabs-dev/keel/tree/main/docs/adr">two short ADRs</a> and a
      <a href="https://github.com/redlinelabs-dev/keel/blob/main/CONTEXT.md">glossary</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="memory">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">memory</span>
      <h2>Engines are swappable. Files stay.</h2>
      <p>Lots of teams are building agent memory, and Claude Code hasn't picked a
      winner. keel's bet is to <strong>own the interface and the files, and treat engines
      as swappable</strong>. The files are markdown you control. The interface is Claude
      Code's documented hooks. An engine turns experience into facts. You can replace
      it, and remembering never depends on it.</p>
      <blockquote>Recall's floor is local file reads. Retain is remote LLM work. Never
      swap them. If you do, your memory stops working whenever your homelab reboots.
      That's a dependency, not a memory system.</blockquote>
      <p>A networked recall can sit above that floor if it has a hard deadline and adds
      nothing when it fails. keel's Hindsight hook works that way: 2 seconds, then
      silence.</p>
      <p>Knowledge is sorted by who can prove it wrong. If <code>grep</code> can, the fact
      is <em>derivable</em>. keel looks it up live and never stores it, so it can't go
      stale. If only you can, it's <em>decided</em>, and the notes store keeps it as a
      pointer plus the reason. If a test suite can, it's <em>logic</em>, and it belongs
      in a package. Code that both projects import beats notes that describe code.</p>
      <p><a href="https://github.com/redlinelabs-dev/keel/blob/main/docs/MEMORY-ARCHITECTURE.md">The
      full architecture →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="absences">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">deliberately not included</span>
      <h2>What's missing is on purpose</h2>
      <ul class="nolist">
        <li>Parsing transcripts, history files, or any undocumented format</li>
        <li>A memory engine, vector store, or LLM call of its own</li>
        <li>A daemon, web UI, or background service</li>
        <li>Credential management</li>
        <li>A second way to share anything an adapter already shares</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="sub">Every guard has an off switch: <code>KEEL_GUARD_OFF</code>,
      <code>KEEL_ACTIVITY_OFF</code>, <code>KEEL_TRAILERS_OFF</code>,
      <code>KEEL_INGEST_OFF</code>, <code>KEEL_RECALL_OFF</code>,
      <code>KEEL_HINDSIGHT_RECALL_OFF</code>. Every write has a backup
      and an undo. A guard that breaks your workflow is a guard you'll eventually turn
      off. So the ingest boundary never blocks, the log never calls a model, and
      <a href="https://github.com/redlinelabs-dev/keel/blob/main/docs/UNINSTALL.md">the whole
      layer comes out in two commands</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
